  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMinimal changes to the overall synoptic pattern have occurred over \nthe past 12 hours as a shortwave ridge remains across the southern \nand central Plains ahead of a strong upper low over the desert \nsouthwest/4-corners region. Another wave tied in closer with the \npolar jet has tried to phase in with the southern cyclone across the \nnorthern Rockies but is expected to continue off into the northern \nUS and southern Canada over the next few days. This should leave the \nsouthern cyclone cutoff from the main flow. A slow east-ward \nprogression of the southern cyclone will continue over the weekend \nwith ample upper-level moisture filtering into the central Plains \nout ahead of the trough axis. Expect dry conditions with scattered \nto broken cirrus decks today and Sunday as temperatures under the \nmid-level ridge warm into the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\nBy Sunday evening, the slow-moving upper low ejects off the central \nRockies and into the central Plains. This should push decent mid-\nlevel and isentropic ascent across western and central Kansas \nincreasing PoPs Sunday night and into Monday afternoon. Some weak \ninstability may be associated within precipitation Monday morning \nthat could produce some isolated thunder, but overall, rain showers \nare the expected precipitation mode. Areas across far eastern Kansas \nwill likely not see too many chances for precipitation with this \nwave as ascent should stay west and then north of these areas.\n\nBy Tuesday and following Monday's upper cyclone northern passage, \nsouthwesterly flow builds across the central US. Some scattered \nnodes of vorticity embedded in the flow are expected to move through \nTuesday morning and with the presence of a 30 knot LLJ, some light \nprecipitation could be noted across eastern Kansas. Forcing should \nquickly weaken through the day leaving most of the area dry. The end \nof the week will see similar set ups for precipitation as weak \nforcing aloft and southerly flow keep low end PoPs for Wednesday and \nThursday (<20%). An upper low passing north of the area should try \nto push a weak boundary through the area late week as well that \ncould again be a better focal point for precipitation. Long range \nensemble guidance for the remainder of October seems to hint at \nabove-average temperatures as mid-level ridging and southwesterly \nflow keeps WAA likely. Will continue to monitor these trends, but \nthis pattern seems very much in line with typical El Nino conditions \nas we head into the winter months.",
